## Redis Guide

Since this project incorporates Redis as a Pub/Sub service, you must adhere to the following instructions before running the app.  
A `docker-compose.yml` file is included in this project which will be used for creating a Redis container. Some commands are also added to the `Makefile` file for running a container (The following guide is applicable if GNU/Make tool is installed on your machine).

### Run A Redis Container

First, make sure Docker is up and running on your machine. Then in the root path of the project run the following command:

```text
$ make docker-up
```

In order to stop the container, you can run the following command:

```text
$ make docker-down
```

Although it stops the container, still the volume related to the previously-created container exits and if you run `make docker-up` again, you have access to persisted data if any.  
In order to stop the container and at the same time remove the volume related to that container, you can run the following command:

```text
$ make docker-down-rm-vol
```

## Publish A New Message

By running the following command in the terminal, open the Redis CLI:

```text
$ redis-cli
```

In the Redis CLI, subscribe to a channel using the `SUBSCRIBE` command. For example, if you want to subscribe to a channel named `testing`, you need to run the following command:

```text
127.0.0.1:6379> SUBSCRIBE testing
```

The generated worker is a single long-running process. Its `main` opens the broker, publishes one sample message on startup, and then subscribes to the channel until you stop it with Ctrl+C:

```go
broker, err := redis.New("testing", logger)
if err != nil {
	logger.Fatal(err)
}
defer func() { _ = broker.Close() }()

// Publish a sample message. The domain entity carries no wire-format tags, so
// the composition root maps it to a transport payload before marshalling.
sample, _ := json.Marshal(struct {
	Data string `json:"data"`
}{Data: "Hello World"})
_ = broker.Produce(ctx, sample)

// Consume every message until the process is stopped.
go broker.Consume(ctx, func(value []byte) {
	logger.Info("message received", "payload", string(value))
})
```

We use the same channel name (`testing`) as the `SUBSCRIBE` command above. Running `make run` publishes one message to the channel; a `redis-cli` subscriber sees:

```text
1) "message"
2) "testing"
3) "{\"data\":\"Hello World\"}"
```

## Publish More Messages

The worker already subscribes to the channel, so to send more messages, publish from the Redis CLI:

```text
127.0.0.1:6379> PUBLISH testing "Hello World"
(integer) 1
```

The terminal window running the worker logs the payload:

```text
message received payload=Hello World
```

## Publisher-Only and Subscriber-Only Workers

To specialize a worker, keep only the half you need in `main`: delete the `broker.Consume(...)` goroutine for a pure publisher, or delete the `broker.Produce(...)` call for a pure subscriber. You can also scaffold two separate workers:

```text
$ gopamin new -n publisher -t worker -b redis
$ gopamin new -n subscriber -t worker -b redis
```

Edit each to keep just its half, then run both with `make run` (start the subscriber first so it doesn't miss the publisher's message). The subscriber logs each payload it receives:

```text
$ make run
message received payload={"data":"Hello World"}
```